I've finally started trying not to just loot shops on my way down but to
drain the shopkeepers of all their money as well (after all, there are
protection tithes to pay). I wonder, has anyone gone through the classes
to see which are the most efficient looters?

To me, the efficiency angle is important. You need to keep your pet from
having to make a lot of trips. Therefore, the prerequisites would seem to
be the ability to walk into a shop with a lot of cash, and to have
lightweight expensive items (or stacks) to sell.

Healers are obviously the best at this. (High starting cash means you can
get lots of credit from the start, plus high charisma means less price
differential between buying and selling your lvl 3 spellbooks.)

Rangers actually have a significant advantage by starting with ~50 +2
arrows. They can be sold in general stores, weapons outlets, *and* antique
armor shops, for Z11 each. Of course, you have to make several drops
and be sure your dog brings them out each time, because with 7-8 charisma,
the repurchase price is Z29 per. Build a stake with the arrows, start
dropping the cash instead, then sell and buy until the shopkeeper is
drained.

Wizards don't do too badly thanks to spellbooks, but the fact they start
with no food can make things awkward. Frankly it's been a while since I
played a wizard--since before discovering the credit trick--so I'm not
sure whether they'd be better than rangers.

Monks have a long hard slog. Oh well, vow of poverty and all that.

These are the only four classes I've played to any extent. Can anyone else
offer commentary about the other fields? 

(I know archaeologists start with a touchstone, but since they no longer
ID gems by sight, I'm not sure they have a class advantage--particularly
if the touchstone is merely uncursed. Maybe A should start with a
touchstone guaranteed to be blessed?)


